2 CONT p. 205) it would seem as if this was Olof Arvidsson Hwijth [1] , one of the progenitors of the noble family Rosenbielke No. 52, at least his letter of nobility was issued on the same day. On Olof Trottesson's family page 206, it m
2 CONC ay be added, although we certainly do not dare to say it, that an Olof Olsson, son of the aforementioned Olof, is at least such an acquaintance of Peder Månsson (fol. 187 f.) and as he was close to the same time as this one,he kno
2 CONC ws a joke. Carl Gustaf Boberg (b. 207), who was ennobled with the name Palmcrona , is said to have become extra ordinary in 1737 and ordinary chancellist in Kammar Kollegium in 1739. This seems difficult to understand, when a few 
2 CONC lines later it is stated that in 1738, 40 he was a member of the Riksdag in the bourgeois estate. May there not be a confusion of similarly named persons, at least Låstbom's statement (Höfd. M. II. p. 98) that Boberg-Palmcrona bec
2 CONC ame mayor of Norrtelje in the 1730s seems more plausible. The expression that he was a member of the Riksens Ständers Commission 1742-46 is misleading, as it can evoke the idea of ​​a Commission working throughout this time, which
2 CONC  did not exist, or it is also understood that he was a member of the Riksens Ständers Commissions, which during these years occurred, which also does not seem to be quite right; but in 1743 he was a member of the commission on the
2 CONC  Finnish war. His nobility of 1751 was not announced and in 1755-56 he is again a member of the Riksdag in the bourgeois class, (according to messages from Prof. CG Malmström). With regard to the information about Staffan Sasse's 
2 CONC marriage (p. 253), it must be remembered that in a Kammar-Archivet's "register of estates, which the nobility snatched from churches and monasteries", his wife is said to be a daughter of Bengt Knutson Ulf . The Skraggenstierna fa
2 CONC mily (p. 271) opens with the familiar legend of the Skraggars' distinguished ancestry, and we learn there that a Scottish king once in ancient times landed on an uninhabited island and was tormented by hunger there. However, there
2 CONC  were wild goats and he promised a reward to whoever caught a buck. This succeeded and the courtiers, who left the goat, got the estate Kragie but also the name Skragge, which is said to mean goat (?). Far be it from us to want to
2 CONC  dispute the credibility of this strange story. We want only as one as a counterpoint to that add an old document, which shows that the Skraggenamnet was known and - who knows - perhaps even arose in Vermland as early as the 15th 
2 CONC century, and this precisely in the areas where the family first appears. In the Chamber Archives there is a witness certificate, given by several farmers on February 28, 1576, about Hällbostads m. fl.tax home, from which the follo
2 CONC wing must be quoted: Thett was enn bonde benemptt Joen Skragga, hann lente enn båth aff enn benemptt Pelle ij Ramstadh oc itt skuttneth oc rowde thenn on Hammaröö schage oc but there were four vats and one hand wheel, and then he 
2 CONC rowed into Staffuichen and laid down on an islet, named Kyllingaholmen, to sleep, and with him saw, the boat hit her from the islet and onto Messeuich ådde, there she broke up. But said the above-mentioned Pelle in Ramstadh to the
2 CONC  foreman Joen: You have now fforuercked 40 marksortich for the hundred solasetts, Therefore he even had the above-mentioned Helbostadhin the sack; thus the same farm came from the treasury and to Peder Larisson. [1] It probably ca
2 CONC nnot be denied that there is a certain agreement between this Jon's sea voyage and the adventure of the Scottish knight Skragge , and it is not unconditionally necessary to go as far as across the sea to get an explanation for the
2 CONC  name Skragge. There is something in the cited certificate that does not seem to belong to the question of Hällbostad's tax or salvation senate, but which, however, does not seem to have been said completely without meaning. The f
2 CONC armers state in passing that Jon took 4 goats and a hand qvarn and this circumstance alone may have been sufficient reason for them to call him "skraggen", [2] which from ancient times was, and still is in many places, one of the 
2 CONC wolf's many nicknames. This surname, like countless others both in older and more recent times, has probably passed on to the offspring and become a family name. [3] This explanation of it The very conclusion here requires an expl
2 CONC anation. This Pelle i Ramstadh , as he writes himself, or Peder Andersson, as he is called in the seal's circumscription, a son of Anders Jonsson (1414-1417) was from the old Vermland Storkeslägten . He appears in documents betwee
2 CONC n 1473-79 and left both Ramstad and Hälbostad to his son Ingemar Pellesson (1480-1519), who again in 1513 sold Ramstad with 2 waste farms to the bishop's chair in Skara. They were born by a paternal cousin, Bronntte Olsson, and fr
2 CONC om him they inherited the above-mentioned Peder Larsson, who was probably his grandson. Peder Larsson, who owned estates both in Vermland and Vestergötland, was married to a daughter of the deceased man Peder Andersson at Brenneba
2 CONC cka , moved to Bro (now Vestbro) and was much used by King Gustaf during his last years; but otherwise a wild rarity. He only had one daughter, married in illegitimacy to a Nils Månsson; and with his nephew Lasse Olsson to Ramstad
2 CONC  and Hjelmarsnäs, court witsman for a banner rider during the time of Carl IX, died in 1606, the family died out on the sword side, but the coat of arms: a stork with a snake in its beak, was adopted unchanged by his grandsons Nil
2 CONC s and Joen Pehrssöner (see noble family Stårck No. 233). [2] That skragge and kraye are fundamentally the same word has seemed to us irrefutable and the information, assistant professor B Richert willingly communicated, has
2 CONT fully confirmed this. Both words originally meant a decrepit, old and therefore unfaithful person. Adjectives and verbs as well as numerous compounds preserve this meaning in the vernacular, such as skragget and krån, skragga and 
2 CONC krå (cf. tvaga and two, slaga and slað , etc.) as well as kråpuk, kråfælling, etc. fl. The evil spirit is also called the old {old Erk in Verml.) the ugly, the sad, the ugly, and so Skraggen came to be a name for him. But now the 
2 CONC wolf is also one of the enemies of man, and therefore the name skraggen, kraggeg was transferred to him, who must not be mentioned by his proper name because "if you call me a wolf, I will make you angry". Skraggen here fully resp
2 CONC onds to the name "the old grey", which is also given to him, and the belief that many wolves are actually changed humans (werewolves) may have contributed to this. [3] The formation of such names on exactly the same grounds contin
2 CONC ues even today, as we sought to reveal in a special essay on names and nicknames among the common people entered in the Swedish Family Journal for the month of May 1876.
