1 OCCU Rådmann og borgermester i Trondheim 1625, rådmann i trondheim
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Christopher Biørnsen (Darre)''' (c. 1567 – May 10, 1642) was one of Trondheim's most prominent, powerful, and affluent public officials during the first half of the 17th century. He left a lasting imprint on the
2 CONC  city's political and economic fabric, serving across several decades as an alderman, burgomaster (mayor), international merchant, and chief guardian of minors.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Origins and Patrician Background ===
2 CONT Christopher was born in Oslo around 1567 (though some modern analyses suggest a slightly later date in the 1570s). He belonged to the historic and influential Darre family, being the son of Oslo alderman Biørn Rolfsen Darre and Ur
2 CONC sulla Trondsdatter. Christopher’s roots were deeply embedded in the traditional administrative elite: both of his grandfathers, Rolf Olufsen Darre and Trond Jonsen, served simultaneously as burgomasters of Oslo to balance civic po
2 CONC wer. Furthermore, his maternal grandfather was burgomaster Biørn Gunnarson of the same city.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Move to Trondheim and Commercial Rise ===
2 CONT As a young man, Christopher relocated to Trondheim to establish his career as a merchant and burgher. His earliest appearance in local records carries a dramatic note: in the 1592 fines and penalties registry (sakefall) for Inderø
2 CONC y, he was penalized for a physical altercation in which he stabbed a man in the arm with a knife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Highly literate and versed in legal documentation, Christopher completed a comprehensive handwritten transcript of Hans Pedersen's Lawbook in Trondheim in 1604. This manuscript was later presented to Lawsinger (lagman) Peder Alfse
2 CONC n in 1637, highlighting Christopher's early access to official council texts either as an alderman or town scribe. Through strategic commercial ventures and his subsequent marriage, he secured a formidable social and economic posi
2 CONC tion. He actively leveraged the massive economic expansion sweeping Trondheim at the turn of the century, establishing major operations in the overseas shipping trades and the highly lucrative Nordland fish trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT === A Stormy and Illustrious Civic Career ===
2 CONT Christopher’s political career was extensive but frequently punctuated by sharp institutional standoffs with the rest of the city administration:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Alderman:''' He is explicitly documented as an alderman (rådmann) for the first time on May 14, 1610, when he participated in the formal royal homage. He appears regularly in council records thereafter, notably in August 1612
2 CONC , and in 1613 when he verified Trondheim's financial and physical contributions to the Swedish Feud (The Kalmar War).
2 CONT * '''The Great Council Dispute (1622–1624):''' In May 1622, Christopher filed a formal appeal to the Diet (herredagen) against a local court ruling that ordered him to pay a fine of 100 daler to the city hospital. Christopher had 
2 CONC stubbornly refused to affix his official seal to a court judgment with which he was entirely unfamiliar and had played no part in deciding. The Diet fully exonerated him, but the case caused a major administrative crisis. Under a 
2 CONC royal decree of 1619, Christopher had been suspended from his council seat while under indictment. Upon his acquittal, the fief lord ordered his immediate reinstatement. However, the existing town council refused to readmit him. I
2 CONC n response, the fief lord subpoenaed the entire city council before the high court. Following a high-profile trial on April 7, 1624, Christopher emerged victorious and successfully reclaimed his seat.
2 CONT * '''Burgomaster:''' Christopher occupied the office of burgomaster (mayor) across multiple non-consecutive terms. He is first recorded as mayor on November 20, 1625, leading a committee of two aldermen and three burghers to estab
2 CONC lish official tariffs for burials and pews inside Nidaros Cathedral. He served as the primary burgomaster in 1626 (noted on April 25). Following Shrovetide in 1627, he rotated back to the rank of alderman, appearing as such in Mar
2 CONC ch 1629. He was re-elected to the mayoral seat by October 19, 1632, when he audited the accounts of churchwarden Oluff Skriver. Holding the status of primary burgomaster at that juncture indicates he had also controlled the office
2 CONC  through 1631. He continued his presence on the council as an alderman and magistrate through 1633.
2 CONT * '''Chief Guardian:''' On July 14, 1635, he was commissioned as the chief guardian of minors (overformynder) for Trondheim. He held this public trust until September 2, 1639, when contemporary guardian protocols note that he asce
2 CONC nded once more to the position of burgomaster. He remained a leader of the city until his death on May 10, 1642.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Wealth and Civic Privileges ===
2 CONT At the time of his passing in 1642, Christopher was an exceptionally wealthy individual. He owned a large urban estate in Trondheim and managed considerable capital, including holding 28 riksdaler in 1638 and 62 riksdaler in 1639 
2 CONC from the boys' home (drengehuset) funds at interest. Furthermore, as a high official, he enjoyed the city's premier economic monopolies. In this era, the town's aldermen and mayors held the exclusive privilege to operate wine cell
2 CONC ars and control wine tavern dispensaries. The immense revenues generated from this monopoly served as the primary financial compensation for their otherwise unpaid public administration of municipal affairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family and Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1590, Christopher married '''Sophie (Sophia) Mentzdatter von Ravensburg'''. She was the daughter of the affluent merchant Mentz von Ravensburg and Margrethe Casparsdotter (a sister of burgomaster Hans Casparsen). Sophie survive
2 CONC d her husband by more than a decade and was buried on March 8, 1653. Together, they had a large family traditionally numbering 9 children, though some genealogical ambiguity exists regarding later lineages.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Verified Children:'''
2 CONT * '''Reverend Mentz Christopherssøn Darre:''' (b. September 30, 1598 – d. 1657). Parish priest of Nidaros Cathedral and a prominent pioneer for early social welfare institutions in Trondheim. He married Maren Pedersdatter Schjeldr
2 CONC up.
2 CONT * '''Rolf Christophersen:''' Identified as Mentz's brother in a 1647 real estate transaction. He married 1) Gisken Hansdatter Bitou (d. 1661) and 2) Maren Jensdatter (widow of Elias instrumentis).
2 CONT * '''Maren Christophersdatter:''' Named in a deed of gift from her aunt, Sara von Ravensburg. She married 1) in 1644 to Johan Grøn, and 2) in 1658 to Johan Fasmer.
2 CONT * '''Hans Christophersen:''' (b. 1601 – d. 1625). Passed away while registered as a student in Copenhagen.
2 CONT * '''Elisabeth Christophersdatter:''' (b. c. 1605 – d. 1685). Married Reverend Ole Jonsen Budde of Snåsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children with Uncertain or Debated Linkages:'''
2 CONT * '''Arsille (Ursilla) Christophersdatter:''' (d. November 20, 1648). Married burgomaster Oluff Jensen Skriver. (Recorded in older lineage charts referenced by Moe).
2 CONT * '''Anna Christophersdatter:''' (d. December 11, 1626). Married Jens Pedersen of Hitra.
2 CONT * '''Steen Christophersen:''' (b. 1610 – d. 1691). Served as the residing chaplain of Stod.
2 CONT * '''Reverend Bjørn Christophersen:''' Parish priest. His estate was probated in 1662. He married Anne Zachariasdatter Holck.
2 CONT * '''Staal Christophersen:''' Highly disputed as a direct son due to his youngest child being born as late as 1660. However, he named his sons Mentz and Ole (the latter became parish priest of Klæbu).
2 CONT * '''Einer Christophersen:''' Listed in contemporary probate registries as "Staal's brother".
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Dahl, Svein Tore (2001): ''Embetsmenn i Midt-Norge i tiden 1536-1660'', pp. 37–38 (section "Christoffer Biørnsen (Darre)"). Based on original documents from the Court Records of the Diet (NHD), local fief accounts, Cathedral reg
2 CONC istries, and the Trondheim guardian protocols. Referenced from images {76F0BCC2-B799-4EF8-8A10-501EBDF8F1C0}.jpg and {921C583A-5A44-4CE5-B851-8514D58D90FE}.jpg.
2 CONT * Schmidt, Olaus (1956): ''Den yngre Darre-slekt: agnatlinjen Darre-Kaarbø'', pp. 14ff.
2 CONT * Grønn, Steinar (1981): ''Karoline og Karl Ludvig Grønn, forfedre og etterkommere''.
2 CONT * Norwegian Journal of Genealogy (NST), Vol. XV and Vol. XXXIII p. 99 ("Slekten Mentzoni i Gildeskål").
